DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ppccaapp__dduummpp__ooppeenn() is called to open a ``savefile'' for  writing.   _f_n_a_m_e
       specifies  the  name  of  the file to open. The file will have the same
       format as those used by ttccppdduummpp(1) and ttccppsslliiccee(1).  If the  file  does
       not exist, it will be created; if the file exists, it will be truncated
       and overwritten.  The name "-" is a synonym for ssttddoouutt.

       ppccaapp__dduummpp__ffooppeenn() is called to write data to an  existing  open  stream
       _f_p;   this   stream   will   be   closed   by   a  subsequent  call  to
       ppccaapp__dduummpp__cclloossee(3).  The stream is assumed to be at the beginning of  a
       file  that  has  been  newly  created or truncated, so that writes will
       start at the beginning of the file.  Note that on Windows, that  stream
       should be opened in binary mode.

       _p  is  a  capture or ``savefile'' handle returned by an earlier call to
       ppccaapp__ccrreeaattee(3) and activated by an earlier call to ppccaapp__aaccttiivvaattee(3), or
       returned by an earlier call to ppccaapp__ooppeenn__oofffflliinnee(3), ppccaapp__ooppeenn__lliivvee(3),
       or ppccaapp__ooppeenn__ddeeaadd(3).  The time stamp precision, link-layer  type,  and
       snapshot  length  from  _p  are used as the link-layer type and snapshot
       length of the output file.

       ppccaapp__dduummpp__ooppeenn__aappppeenndd() is like ppccaapp__dduummpp__ooppeenn() but, if the  file  al‐
       ready  exists,  and is a pcap file with the same byte order as the host
       opening the file, and has the same  time  stamp  precision,  link-layer
       header type, and snapshot length as _p, it will write new packets at the
       end of the file.

